  "operator_census": {
    "exposed_class": "4/4 use `!==` (case inequality)",
    "semantics": "`!==` returns 1 whenever the operands differ in any of the four states, so an x expectation always fails the check. `!=` and `==` return x when either operand is x, and `if (x)` takes the false branch, so an x expectation silently passes.",
    "library_wide": "Template-level inspection (every distinct oracle template read): `!==` is the check operator in all templates examined (comparator, 74181 case table, memory pattern, passive-switch topology, counter expression, 7-segment font, keycode). The only `==`-against-expected idiom found is tb_ttl_74x962.sv:28 `if (q_a == q_b) errors++;`, which asserts that two complementary outputs differ - not an x-tolerant pass. A machine-counted per-file operator census requires running classify.py."
  },
  "risk_notes": [
    "The exposed class is exactly the 74x677/678/679/680 comparator family; no other testbench in the library puts a for loop inside a function.",
    "Strict `!==` catches the x mode but not a stale-but-legal return: a stale expectation that happens to equal the DUT output passes. That is the only silent-masking path in this class, and it needs coincidence.",
    "All four comparator TBs call the oracle only 16-20k times (131072 for 679), while 677/678/680 call it ~1-2k times, so exposure scales with vector count."
  ]
